      {
        "label": "3(c)(i)",
        "question": "Describe how (a) age and (b) gender impact on a person's choice of leisure activities.",
        "marks": 12,
        "model": "Age: physical ability and safety vary; interests change from teenage IT to gardening in later life, and disposable income may differ. Gender: some sports/crafts are dominated by one sex; males may have more leisure time and tend to participate more in sport.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 11,
        "scheme_points": 4,
        "model_source": "scheme"
      },
      {
        "label": "3(c)(ii)",
        "question": "Name and evaluate two leisure facilities available in your community. Refer to: cost and value for money; the range of facilities offered.",
        "marks": 18,
        "model": "Two leisure facilities in my community are the municipal sports and leisure centre and the public library/community arts centre.\n\nThe municipal sports centre charges either a pay-as-you-go admission or a monthly/annual membership. Reduced rates may be available for students, families, unemployed people and older adults. Membership is good value for a frequent user because one fee can cover the swimming pool, gym and some classes; a casual rate is better for someone attending infrequently. However, joining fees, specialist lessons and equipment or sports clothing add to the cost. Value also depends on convenient opening hours, cleanliness, qualified supervision, disability access and whether overcrowding restricts actual use.\n\nIts range is broad: swimming lanes and lessons, children's pool, gym and fitness assessment, exercise classes such as aerobics, circuits, yoga or Pilates, indoor courts for basketball and badminton, outdoor pitches, changing rooms and sometimes a café or club rooms. This variety supports fitness, weight control, skill development and social contact for different ages. The disadvantages are travel time for rural users, possible waiting lists and the risk that expensive equipment is poor value if a member rarely attends.\n\nThe public library/community arts centre is free to join or costs very little, so it gives excellent value and is accessible to low-income families. Borrowing books, e-books and many digital resources is normally free; printing, late returns, performances or specialist courses may carry small charges. It avoids the cost of buying books, newspapers, internet access or entertainment separately. Value is high where opening hours, public transport, trained staff and disability access are good, although limited evening hours, small collections or booked-out events can reduce usefulness.\n\nIts facilities can include adult and children's lending libraries, study desks, newspapers, computers, Wi-Fi, local-history resources, literacy support, homework clubs, toddler story sessions, book clubs, exhibitions, talks, music or drama rooms and low-cost art, craft or language classes. These develop intellectual and creative skills, provide quiet study, encourage reading and bring people together. It offers little vigorous physical exercise, so it complements rather than replaces the sports centre.\n\nOverall, the sports centre gives the best value to a regular active user, while the library/arts centre provides the widest affordable educational and cultural access. A family's choice should reflect members' ages and interests, total fees and equipment costs, distance, opening times, safety, accessibility and how often the facilities will genuinely be used.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 11,
        "scheme_points": 10,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
