        "question": "Give an account of (i) the nutritive value and (ii) dietetic value of cheese.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Nutritive value: cheese is an excellent source of high-biological-value protein containing all essential amino acids. This supports growth, repair and production of enzymes, hormones and antibodies. Most full-fat cheese contains considerable saturated fat and cholesterol, providing concentrated heat and energy but requiring moderation because excess raises obesity and coronary-heart-disease risk. Cheese contains very little carbohydrate because much lactose drains away in the whey during manufacture. It supplies vitamin A for healthy skin and vision and B-group vitamins, especially riboflavin and B12. It is rich in calcium and phosphorus for bones and teeth and also supplies some sodium, particularly where salt is added for flavour and preservation. It is deficient in vitamin C, iron and fibre. Dietetic value: cheese is compact, versatile, convenient and generally easy to digest because its protein has been coagulated and partly changed during ripening. It is valuable for children, teenagers, pregnant women and older people because protein, calcium and phosphorus support growth and bone maintenance. Its many varieties, flavours and textures can increase appetite and it can enrich sandwiches, sauces, salads and cooked dishes. Reduced-fat and reduced-salt varieties suit some special diets. However, portions of full-fat, salty cheese should be controlled. It should be combined with wholegrain carbohydrate, vegetables or fruit to supply fibre, vitamin C and iron and create a balanced meal.",
