    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "(a)",
        "question": "Explain how the food pyramid is used to plan healthy balanced meals.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "The food pyramid converts healthy-eating advice into the types and relative amounts of food needed each day. A meal plan should take most foods from the large lower shelves and very little from the small top shelf, while portion number and size are adjusted for age, sex, health and activity. Fruit, vegetables and salad should provide about five to seven portions daily and should appear at every meal because they supply fibre, water, vitamins, minerals and antioxidants. Wholemeal cereals, breads, potatoes, pasta and rice provide the main source of carbohydrate and energy; high-fibre varieties are chosen most often. Milk, yoghurt and cheese provide HBV protein, calcium and vitamins, with lower-fat choices generally suitable for adults. Meat, poultry, fish, eggs, beans and nuts supply protein, iron and other micronutrients; include lean choices and fish regularly, while pulses provide economical plant protein. Fats, spreads and oils are used sparingly, with small amounts of mono- or polyunsaturated oils preferred. Foods and drinks high in fat, sugar and salt are not needed for health and should be occasional rather than daily. Water is the main drink, regular meals are planned, and cooking methods such as steaming, grilling and baking avoid added fat. By varying foods within every shelf and combining the shelves across breakfast, lunch, dinner and snacks, the pyramid helps achieve energy balance and prevents both deficiency and excess.",
        "diagram": "exam-images/home-economics/2026-OL-Q3-a.png",
        "source_page": 3,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "(b)",
        "question": "Describe three lifestyle guidelines to follow to maintain good health.",
        "marks": 15,
        "model": "Regular physical activity is essential. An adult should build activity into daily life through brisk walking, cycling, sport or active housework and include weight-bearing and muscle-strengthening exercise. Activity improves cardiovascular fitness, maintains muscle and bone, supports energy balance and weight control, reduces stress and may increase protective HDL cholesterol. A second guideline is to protect mental health through adequate sleep, rest and stress management. A regular sleep routine, realistic workload, leisure, supportive relationships and relaxation prevent exhaustion and improve concentration, mood and immunity. Third, avoid tobacco and limit alcohol. Smoking damages the lungs and blood vessels and greatly increases cancer and coronary-heart-disease risk; avoiding both smoking and second-hand smoke is safest. Alcohol supplies empty calories and excess harms the liver, judgement and relationships, so it should never be used to manage stress. These habits should accompany regular balanced meals, adequate water, good hygiene and appropriate health checks. Together they reduce the risk of obesity, type 2 diabetes, hypertension and heart disease while supporting long-term physical, social and emotional wellbeing.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 3,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      },
      {
        "label": "(c)",
        "question": "Outline three factors that affect the energy requirements of an adult.",
        "marks": 15,
        "model": "Body size, age and sex influence basal metabolic rate. A larger or more muscular adult has more tissue to maintain and usually needs more energy. Men often have more lean muscle than women and therefore a higher requirement. BMR and muscle mass generally fall with age, so an older adult usually needs less energy than a younger adult of similar size. Physical activity is a second major factor. A construction worker, athlete or person who walks and cycles daily uses much more energy for muscular work than an office worker with a sedentary routine; the intensity and duration of activity both matter. Physiological and health circumstances are a third factor. Pregnancy and lactation require extra energy to support fetal growth and milk production. Fever, illness, recovery from injury or surgery may raise metabolic demand, whereas immobility lowers expenditure. Climate can also modify need because energy is used to maintain body temperature in cold conditions. Energy intake should therefore be matched to BMR and activity so input equals output, maintaining a healthy weight rather than causing weight loss or adipose storage.",
        "diagram": "",
        "source_page": 3,
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
