        "label": "(a)",
        "question": "Describe three ways older people can maintain their independence.",
        "marks": 15,
        "model": "First, an older person can adapt the home to remain safe and manageable: install handrails, ramps, non-slip flooring, a level-access shower, good lighting and a personal alarm; use a downstairs bedroom or stairlift if mobility falls. Labour-saving appliances and home-help services reduce workload while allowing the person to retain control. Second, maintaining physical and social health supports independence. Balanced meals, regular suitable exercise, medical checks and correct medication preserve strength; clubs, hobbies, family contact and community groups prevent isolation and protect emotional wellbeing. Third, the person can organise practical and financial supports. A realistic budget, pension and entitlements such as a medical card or free travel help meet needs. Public transport, community transport, Meals on Wheels, home-support services and visits from a public-health nurse enable daily life without institutional care. Learning to use a phone, alarm or online service can improve communication and access, but support should respect the older person’s choices, dignity and remaining abilities rather than creating unnecessary dependence.",
